[[hybrid-retrieval]]
Hybrid retrieval

Hybrid retrieval runs two complementary searchers over the same corpus and merges
their results. A sparse lexical searcher matches exact terms and identifiers, while
a dense searcher matches meaning through learned vector representations. Neither is
strictly better: the lexical side anchors precision on rare tokens, and the dense
side recovers relevant passages that share no words with the query.

The two ranked lists are combined with Reciprocal Rank Fusion. Each document earns a
fused score equal to the sum over retrievers of one divided by a small constant plus
its rank in that retriever. Because the formula consumes only ranks, it needs no
score normalization and no training, which is why it is the default way to blend a
keyword list with a vector list. The result reliably beats either retriever alone.
